    id:'opqb-t28-47', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The following statement about visual impairment caused by cataract is false:',
    options:['"second sight" is caused by lenticular myopia and improves near vision','Mild posterior subcapsular cataracts never cause visual symptoms','Nuclear cataract impairs reading more than cortical cataract','Cataract can cause Ambylopia'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**A false-statement stem with two near-misses in it, so read the absolutes.** The word that condemns option B is **"never"**, and the word that condemns option C — the runner-up — is **"more than"**. Only one of them is flatly contradicted by the deck, and it is B.\n\n**Why B is the key.** `L18) Cataract II` characterises posterior subcapsular cataract as an opacity sitting at the **posterior capsule, near the nodal point of the eye**, which is why it punches above its size: it **"appears dark against the red reflex"**, it produces **glare prominent when opacities are** central, and it is **aggravated by miosis** — bright light and near work both constrict the pupil and drive the remaining clear aperture straight through the plaque. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints the same clinical point in the patient’s own terms: patients with posterior subcapsular cataracts **"may note a relatively"** early difficulty with near work. So a *mild* PSC is precisely the cataract that **does** cause symptoms while acuity still looks respectable. "Never" is untenable.\n\n**Why each distractor is true, and therefore wrong here:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **"second sight"** | **True.** `L18` prints **"‘Second sight’: elderly regain near vision"** and its own self-test answers *"Nuclear cataract → DISTANT vision (myopic shift). Patient may read better (‘second sight’)."* `L7,8) Refractive errors` files the mechanism under **index myopia** — "increase refractive index of the lens nucleus (nuclear cataract)". A myopic shift does improve near vision. |\n| **Nuclear impairs reading more than cortical** | ⚠️ The trap that looks false and is not. It reads like the opposite of second sight — but the printed comparison is between **nuclear and cortical**, and `L18` gives cortical **"impact on VA depends on proximity to"** the visual axis, i.e. peripheral cortical spokes may spare reading entirely for years while a browning nucleus eventually degrades everything. As a blanket comparison it is defensible; as an absolute it is not the *most* false statement on offer. |\n| **Cataract can cause Ambylopia** | **True**, and the mechanism is the deck’s own: `L18` lists **"Dense congenital cataract — prevent amblyopia (within weeks)"** among surgical indications, and the paediatric row of its cataract table gives **"VA loss + amblyopia risk"**. A cataract present during the sensitive period is a classic **deprivation** amblyopia. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Match the opacity to the complaint: nucleus ⇒ distance blurs and a myopic shift; cortex ⇒ glare and spokes, acuity spared until the axis is reached; **PSC ⇒ near vision and glare first, out of all proportion to the Snellen line**. \n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Reject "never" as an absolute about mild posterior subcapsular cataract, and hold the three true statements — second sight, the nuclear/cortical reading comparison, and deprivation amblyopia — as true.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q47 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired: option D prints "Ambylopia" for amblyopia, capitalised mid-sentence; option A opens lower-case inside straight double quotes. Both kept as printed. Supporting: `L18) Cataract II` (second sight; nuclear/cortical/PSC comparison table; PSC dark against the red reflex; congenital cataract and amblyopia); `L7,8) Refractive errors` (index myopia); `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` (PSC and near work).'
